<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Westphalia</h1><p class="st-meta">1180 CE – 1803 CE · capital Arnsberg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Ducal territory in the Sauerland held for six centuries by the archbishops of Cologne after the fall of Henry the Lion.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a> was deposed, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelnhausen-charter">Gelnhausen Charter</a> of 1180 split his <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony</a>. The western part, with the title Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angria">Engern</a>, went to Archbishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-i-archbishop-of-cologne">Philip of Heinsberg</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-cologne">Cologne</a>. The archbishops turned this title into real rule over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sauerland">Sauerland</a>, buying the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-of-arnsberg">County of Arnsberg</a> in 1368.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Ruled by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/electorate-of-cologne">Electorate of Cologne</a> as a separate territory, the duchy had its own estates and diet meeting at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnsberg">Arnsberg</a>, with an electoral governor there. The land was hilly and rural, known for iron working and forestry. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne's</a> ambitions in the region were checked by its defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-worringen">Worringen</a> in 1288 and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soest-feud">Soest Feud</a>, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soest">Soest</a> broke away to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kleve">Cleves</a>. The duchy stayed Catholic throughout.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1803 the duchy was secularised and given to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-hesse">Hesse-Darmstadt</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> transferred it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1816, and it became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/province-of-westphalia">Prussian Province of Westphalia</a>; it now lies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-westphalia.jpg" alt="Duchy of Westphalia" width="320" height="205"><figcaption>Image: NNW · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herzogtum_Westfalen_und_Umland_etwa_1645.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1180 CE – 1803 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Arnsberg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Philip of Heinsberg, Clemens August of Bavaria</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Westphalian Low German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Herzogtum Westfalen</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1492&amp;focus=duchy-of-westphalia" aria-label="Duchy of Westphalia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4863 398 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Westphalia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5183 654L5162 673M5183 654L5198 698"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5162 673h0M5198 698h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5183 654h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5183 654h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Arnsberg. <a href="/atlas?year=1492&amp;focus=duchy-of-westphalia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
